Hello all,
I am trying remove the effects of specimen bending in my landmarks by removing the PC that describes the bending, and then backtransforming to get adjusted coordinates, as in these papers: https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1558-5646.2007.00259.x,  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1558-5646.2012.01622.x.  This is my first time attempting this, but the way I've found <https://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/229092/how-to-reverse-pca-and-reconstruct-original-variables-from-several-principal-com> to do it seems to be: X = Z•t(V) + mean(V) where X is the backtransformed data, Z is the PCs (minus the PC being removed), and V is the eigenvectors (minus the one being removed).

My question is how do I get V from the gm.prcomp output, if this is possible? I initially expected from the documentation that V would be the $rotation matrix, but it has a different number of columns from $x, so Z and V are non-conformable and can't be multiplied.

For reference, my landmark array is [24 x 2 x 51], $x is [51 x 46], and $rotation is [48 x 48], and the line I am trying to use to execute the above formula is: pca$x%*%t(pca$rotation)+rowMeans(pca$rotation).

thanks very much in advance,
Grant

From uaab55242ff4da6c6 at groups.google.com  Fri Jan 22 20:50:45 2021
From: uaab55242ff4da6c6 at groups.google.com (Mike Collyer)
